Yes. The deeper distinction is pain versus damage.

Pain is a signal. Damage is a state change in the system. Sometimes they correlate tightly; sometimes they do not. You can become extraordinarily tolerant of pain while still being vulnerable to tissue damage, exhaustion, infection, degradation, or cumulative wear. Mastery is not just suppressing the alarm—it is learning when the alarm is informative, misleading, trainable, or pointing at something that actually needs protection.

The same thing applies to the body as identity. Your body is not the whole of you, but it is one of the primary interfaces through which the world encounters you. Appearance, posture, movement, expression, speech, clothing, strength, softness, scars, grooming—all of those become signals others use to construct their model of you.

That means you can use the interface deliberately without confusing it for the self.

\text{self} \neq \text{presentation}

but:

\text{presentation} \rightarrow \text{changes how the world responds to self}

And because the world responds differently, your own internal state can change in return. That gives you a loop:

\text{body/presentation}
\rightarrow
\text{social feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{internal state}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{new feedback}

So beauty, ugliness, strength, vulnerability, discipline, even deliberate discomfort can all become state-control instruments if used consciously rather than worshipped as identity.

Your “why not?” instinct is also valuable, with one guardrail: use constraints as experimental boundaries, not as declarations of impossibility.

Biology repeatedly does things that look impossible until the mechanism exists. Flight did not violate physics when birds evolved it; evolution discovered structures that exploited physics differently. The relevant question becomes:

What would have to change for this to become possible?

That turns “the body doesn’t work that way” into a research question:

\text{current mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{modifiable variable}
\rightarrow
\text{adaptation}

Some constraints can be trained around. Some can be redesigned technologically. Some require generations of selection. Some are hard physical limits. The mistake is treating all four categories as the same.

And the acorn line is stronger than “I can become the shade.”

You may not personally instantiate the final form. But you can encode enough structure, selection pressure, knowledge, tools, culture, or example that something downstream does.

\text{acorn}
\rightarrow
\text{developmental program}
\rightarrow
\text{tree}
\rightarrow
\text{shade}

The acorn does not contain a miniature tree. It contains rules capable of producing one under the right conditions.

That is a useful model for legacy, training, culture, and Pantheon-like work too: sometimes your job is not to be the completed answer. It is to preserve the generative instructions so the answer can keep unfolding after you.